


“We were outside your house.”




“You said you needed to check something.”




“The audio level.”




“And I got out.”




“Do you remember crossing the street?”




He looked at the floor.




“Do you remember coming upstairs?”




“No.”




Maren took her phone from her pocket. “I’m calling someone.”




“No.”




“You’re bleeding and you don’t remember how you got here.”




“I’m not going to a hospital so they can ask whether I heard voices.”




“Then don’t tell them you heard voices.”




“I didn’t.”




The answer came quickly. His gaze returned to the camera on the floor, though the lens faced away.




Maren kept the phone in her hand. “What did you hear?”




He pressed his lips together.




“On the drive, you said the sound came back after we left the preserve.”




“I said I kept hearing it.”




“That’s different.”




“I know.”




“Did you hear it here?”




“No.”




“Did you see anyone?”




“No.”




“Did you take something?”




His head lifted. “No.”




“I have to ask.”




“I know what you have to ask.”




He drew his injured hand toward himself. The notebook shifted under it. Maren saw writing across the open page.




I DON’T WANT TO GO BACK.




The sentence filled the first line. It appeared again below it, then again, descending the page in uneven rows. Some letters were narrow and upright. Others slanted so sharply that they crossed the line above. The final repetition had torn the paper beneath the word BACK.




Maren looked at his hand, then the page.




“When did you write this?”




Jonah followed her gaze. For several seconds he did not move.




“I didn’t.”




“It’s your notebook.”




“I know.”




“It’s your handwriting.”




“I know.”




“Were you writing in the car?”




“I was holding it.”




“That isn’t what I asked.”




He closed the notebook.




Maren heard the plastic outside snap against the upper frame. Jonah flinched and raised his injured hand before he caught himself.




“That’s the house wrap,” she said. “It’s been doing that since I got out of the car.”




“I know what it is.”




“Good.”




“You always know what it was after.”




He spoke quietly. Maren’s shoulders tightened, but she stayed crouched. “I’m trying to find out whether you need medical help.”




“And I’m telling you this started there.”




“At Harrow River.”




His eyes fixed on hers. “It started before we left.”




He stressed before. Maren could not tell whether he meant that something had begun at the preserve or that he had begun connecting his fear to it before they left.




“What started?”




“I got angry.”




“You were scared.”




“I was angry before I was scared. You asked whether we should keep going, and I wanted you to stop talking. Not because you were wrong.” He looked down at the notebook. “Because if you said we should leave, I knew I’d leave.”




“That sounds like being scared.”




“It didn’t feel like fear.”




“What did it feel like?”




“Like I had one thought and you were in the way of it.”




Maren said nothing.




Jonah had snapped at her near the river crossing. That part she remembered. He had gone ahead on the trail while she checked the map. When she caught up, he stood under a cottonwood listening toward the water. He apologized in the car, then apologized again twenty minutes later as if he had forgotten the first time.




He opened the notebook with his left hand. “I wrote down what happened when we got back.”




The repeated sentence covered three pages.




Maren reached for the notebook. Jonah held it for a moment, then released it.




The first page began in his ordinary field notes: trail conditions, parking access, the distance from the shelter to the river, places where a tripod would fit. Halfway down, the handwriting tightened.




heard it west of trail




Maren said move




didn’t want her voice




wanted to keep walking




The next line had been crossed out until the paper had thinned.




Below it:




not following




keeping out




The repeated sentence began at the bottom margin.




I DON’T WANT TO GO BACK.




On the second page, the line appeared nineteen times. On the third, the wording changed once.




I DON’T WANT HER TO GO BACK.




Maren closed the notebook.




“You remember writing the field notes?”




“In the car. Before we left the county.”




“And these?”




“No.”




“Did you sleep at all?”




“Maybe twenty minutes.”




“You’ve been awake since yesterday morning.”




“I know.”




“You hit something hard enough to split your hand.”




“I know.”




“You walked into a construction site and don’t remember crossing the street.”




“I know.”




Each answer came softer.




Maren put the notebook beside her knee. “Then we stick to what we know. We clean your hand. You stay with me tonight. Tomorrow we talk to a doctor.”




Jonah shook his head.




“That’s not optional.”




“I’m not going to tell a doctor the woods changed me.”




“You don’t have to. You tell them you haven’t slept, you lost track of several minutes, and you injured your hand.”




“And the footage?”




Maren glanced at the camera.




“What footage?”




“You recorded in the car.”




“You asked me to.”




“I know.”




“I recorded while I searched for you.”




“I saw.”




“No one else has seen it.”




“Yet.”




Jonah did not accuse her. He did not need to. Maren had edited interviews with frightened homeowners, grieving parents, and embarrassed witnesses into sequences with clean beginnings and sharp endings. She had cut around pauses, removed repeated questions, raised low audio, and placed reaction shots where viewers would understand them. She had never invented an event. She also knew how much meaning she could create without inventing one.




She picked up the camera, opened the card compartment, and removed the memory card. The piece of plastic was warm from the camera body. She put it in the zippered pocket inside her jacket.




“This does not go into the episode unless you watch it later and agree.”




Jonah looked at the pocket. “You need it to understand what happened.”




“I do need it to understand what happened. That doesn’t mean anyone else gets to see it.”




“What if it proves—”




“Then we’ll look at it tomorrow.”




“You don’t know that’s all.”




“No.”




He turned his injured hand palm up. Blood had begun to dry across the knuckles. “You didn’t see me before.”




“I saw you at the preserve.”




“You saw me after the sound.”




Maren said nothing.




“You saw me trying not to leave.”




Maren stood. Her knees hurt from crouching on the subfloor. “We are leaving now.”




Jonah did not move.




She held out her hand, then switched and offered her left so he would not have to use the injured one.




He stared at it.




“I’m not filming,” she said.




He took her hand.




Maren helped him stand. He swayed once and caught a wall stud with his shoulder. She waited until his balance returned, then guided him into the hall. At the stairs he stopped and looked through the open framing toward the street.




Their car waited below with both passenger-side doors open. Its interior light cast a weak rectangle across the curb. From the second floor, the distance between the car and the house looked longer than it had when Maren crossed it.
